ts covering all types  of industries and functions I have been  involved in large M&amp;A mergers and  acquisitions transactions I've been  working on major cost and revenue  transformations of large groups of  companies I've worked on all kinds of  countries within an outside of Europe I  work together with buying departments  helping them improve their direct and  indirect spend I developed new sales  strategies for large chemicals companies  new pricing structures for retail  companies evaluated the capex pipeline  for automotive suppliers and many many  many more things I would argue that is  just impossible to get that amount of  experience in that little time if we  work in pretty much any regular industry  role and last but not least be exposure  to sea level management of large budget  companies that you get early on on many  projects we work closely together with  CEOs CFOs  of large companies we work together with  business unit heads with really senior  level management of the companies these  are people that if you work in a normal  department  and Industry royal might never even  speak to face-to-face in the first years  of your career and to the contrary as a  McKinsey o BCG consultant you sit  together in a room with them and work  together with them on the most pressing  top-of-mind issues that these c-level  executives have in their head and they  seek your expertise they respect you and  they closely work together with you to  solve these high priority issues of  their companies this is just  unparalleled this will not be possible  in most industry roads so looking at  these five reasons I honestly believe  that pretty much independent of what you  want to do in the long term you will  benefit from having worked a couple of  years in a large strategy consulting  firm such as McKinsey or BCG but also do  not get me wrong consulting is not  perfect it's not a fairy tale it's no  lala land that you work in and  everything is just great and amazing  there are some significant downsides of  working in consulting as well and here  I'm not even talking about work-life  balance but rather specific learnings  that I believe are crucial and  absolutely needed for you to be  successful in the long term but which  you will not develop at all in  consulting so this could actually be  another video me talking about what the  things are that you will for sure not  learn in consulting so if this is  something that would interest you please  let me know below in the comments and I  likely make another video on that in the  couple of weeks but just because there  are some things which you will not learn  in consulting I also believe that it is  important that after a couple of years  you are thinking about making a  transition and going into a more  operational more hands-on role your  development path of becoming a good  well-versed business professional of  becoming a good general manager would  just not be completed if you're just  working your consulting you need to  small operational line management  experience this way so what is your  thinking on all of this let me know your  opinion below in the comments probably  some of you thinking that I'm wrong and  consulting is not the best role to star  to see young business professionals so  very happy to have an open conversation  here let me know what your thoughts are  and I will give you an answer below in  the comments and again if you took any  value out of this video at all please  hit the like button for the YouTube  algorithm and also hit the  right button as well as activate the  notification there to stay informed and  up-to-date on all the future content I  have planned for you I will release at  least one video every single Saturday  and as some of you have already seen I'm  pretty active on my Instagram and  posting new content every single day  there so follow me on my Instagram  handle firm learning to stay posted  thank you for watching everybody stay  safe and keep learning this is highly  firm firm learning
 

